<main id="main">
<section class="hero" id="top"><div class="w">
<p class="kick">Self-initiated concept · Version 2.0</p>
<h1>See your footprint.<br>Know your next move.</h1>
<p style="font-size:1.2rem;margin:22px 0 28px">EarthPulse AI turns environmental data into one Earth score and shows the single action that would improve it most. Version 2.0 redesigns my first concept around that idea.</p>
<div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:24px"><a class="btn p" href="#prototype">Try the prototype</a><a class="btn" href="#problem">Read the case study</a></div>
<p class="note">Concept project. All numbers are illustrative sample data, not real user or environmental data.</p>
<dl class="sum"><div><dt>Project</dt><dd>EarthPulse AI</dd></div><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>UX/UI designer, solo</dd></div><div><dt>Version 1</dt><dd>2 months, Figma</dd></div><div><dt>Version 2</dt><dd>Interactive prototype built with Claude AI</dd></div><div><dt>Platform</dt><dd>Web and mobile</dd></div></dl>
</div></section>

<section id="problem"><div class="w">
<p class="kick">Problem</p>
<p class="quote">Climate anxiety is high. Action is low. The gap isn’t motivation, it’s feedback.</p>
<div class="g3"><div class="card"><h3>The problem</h3><p>People can’t see how everyday choices add up, so they don’t know what to change.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>The idea</h3><p>Explain environmental data in plain language and turn it into one clear next step.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>The vision</h3><p>One place where anyone can see their impact and know what to do next.</p></div></div>
<div class="head" style="margin-top:56px"><h2>Problems I set out to solve</h2><p>From my review of existing sustainability tools in version 1, and how version 2 answers each.</p></div>
<ul class="map">
<li><b>Data opacity</b><span>Every score has a “Why this?” explanation, and the coach explains how it is calculated.</span></li>
<li><b>No actionability</b><span>A “Your next best action” card sits next to the score on the dashboard.</span></li>
<li><b>Fragmented data</b><span>One score and one dashboard across transport, energy, water and waste.</span></li>
<li><b>Poor mobile UX</b><span>The dashboard has a mobile layout. Use the Mobile toggle in the prototype.</span></li>
<li><b>No social layer</b><span>Community stays a product area (leaderboard, team challenges). Its screens are not designed yet.</span></li>
<li><b>Boring interface</b><span>One deep green, clear data colours and plain language instead of dense charts.</span></li></ul>
</div></section>

<section class="alt" id="users"><div class="w">
<div class="g2" style="align-items:start"><div class="card"><p class="kick" style="margin:0 0 8px">Concept persona</p><h3 style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:10px">Maya</h3>
<p>A concept persona based on my review of existing tools. Not built from interviews and not validated with real users.</p>
<p style="margin-top:12px"><b>Wants:</b> to know whether her everyday choices matter, and what to do about it.</p><p style="margin:0"><b>Gets frustrated by:</b> data without explanation, and tools that make her do all the work.</p></div>
<div><h2>Where the product helps Maya</h2><p style="color:var(--soft);margin-top:12px">The riskiest moment is connecting accounts: it asks for the most trust before she has seen any value. Onboarding in the prototype shows what is read, what never is, and gives her a score before asking for more.</p></div></div>
<ol class="jr"><li><h3>Discovers</h3><p>Hears about it from a colleague.</p><em>Plain-language promise</em></li><li><h3>Visits the site</h3><p>Reads features, watches the demo.</p><em>One idea, no jargon</em></li><li><h3>Connects data</h3><p>Links accounts and sources.</p><em>Say what is read and what isn’t</em></li><li><h3>First dashboard</h3><p>Sees score and a recommendation.</p><em>Score plus one next action</em></li><li><h3>Week three</h3><p>Daily check-ins and goals.</p><em>Goals keep the habit going</em></li></ol>
</div></section>

<section id="prototype"><div class="w">
<div class="head"><p class="kick">Prototype</p><h2>Try version 2.0</h2><p>Connect sources, see a score, add the recommended action and watch the score and next action change. The coach uses scripted replies; there is no live AI behind it.</p></div>
<div class="ctl"><div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Screen"><button data-s="onboarding" aria-pressed="true">Onboarding</button><button data-s="dashboard" aria-pressed="false">Dashboard</button><button data-s="coach" aria-pressed="false">AI Coach</button></div>
<div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Layout"><button data-v="desk" aria-pressed="true">Desktop</button><button data-v="mob" aria-pressed="false">Mobile</button></div></div>
<div class="stage"><div class="frame" id="frame"><div class="ft"><strong>EarthPulse AI</strong><span class="sub">Sample data</span></div><div class="fb" id="app" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class="alt" id="decisions"><div class="w">
<div class="head"><h2>Key decisions</h2></div>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Why</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>One Earth score (0–100)</td><td>A quick read answers “how am I doing?” before any detail.</td><td>Hides detail, so the breakdown sits one step down.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Next best action beside the score</td><td>A recommendation is more useful than another chart.</td><td>Needs a visible reason, so every action has a “Why this?”.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Three-step onboarding</td><td>Short setup lowers the chance people quit before seeing value.</td><td>Needs clear reassurance about data, so the privacy panel comes first.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deep green with separate data colours</td><td>Calm, readable, and quick to scan: blue for water, amber for waste.</td><td>Colour is never the only signal, so values and labels always appear.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="head" style="margin-top:56px"><h2>Product structure</h2><p>The same five areas as version 1.</p></div>
<div class="g5" id="ia"></div>
</div></section>

<section id="system"><div class="w">
<div class="head"><h2>Design system</h2><p>Kept small on purpose: one green, neutral text, three data colours, an 8px spacing scale. Primary green on the off-white background is about 6:1 contrast.</p></div>
<div class="sw"><div><i style="background:#166A43"></i><span>Green #166A43</span></div><div><i style="background:#12301F"></i><span>Forest #12301F</span></div><div><i style="background:#EAF3E6"></i><span>Tint #EAF3E6</span></div><div><i style="background:#2B7BB9"></i><span>Water #2B7BB9</span></div><div><i style="background:#B9800F"></i><span>Waste #B9800F</span></div><div><i style="background:#14231B"></i><span>Text #14231B</span></div></div>
<div class="g2"><div class="card"><h3 style="font-size:2rem;font-weight:800">Plus Jakarta Sans</h3><p>Headings and key numbers. Body and UI text in Inter, 16px minimum.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div style="display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px"><button class="btn p sm">Primary</button><button class="btn sm">Secondary</button></div><div class="bar2"><i style="width:62%"></i></div><p>Components use 16px radius, 24px padding and 44px minimum tap targets.</p></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class="alt" id="next"><div class="w g2" style="align-items:start">
<div><h2>What changed from version 1</h2><ul class="l" style="margin-top:20px"><li>Removed the invented stats, testimonials and unproven research claims.</li><li>Added a next best action, onboarding with a privacy panel, an AI Coach and a mobile dashboard.</li><li>Fixed contradictory dashboard data, added units and consistent labels.</li><li>Cut the repeated hero and dashboard, and the stock photos.</li></ul></div>
<div><h2>What I would test next</h2><ul class="l" style="margin-top:20px"><li>Can a new user connect a source and see a score quickly?</li><li>Do users understand what the Earth score means?</li><li>Which suggested action do people choose first?</li><li>Honest limits: not yet tested with users. Community and goals screens, the landing page and the score logic are not part of this version.</li></ul></div>
</div></section>
</main>
